     I think we might do well to differentiate between different TYPES of
dogs and cats as well as just the experience.  For instance, we might al-
low different classes to select what type of dog or cat they were to take.
Cats and dogs would still gain xp and hp, but fer chrissake, we really need
a ceiling on that sort of thing.  Having a 350hp cat doesn't overbalance
the game particularly, but it's not very realistic.  I'd hate to have to
fight one of them things on a bones level.
     Another thing to figure in would be the move_pet option, which mightr
(might) only function with smaller dogs and cats (obviously, more cats than
dogs).
     I thought this would fit in well with:>
:A cat would never get in your way in a hallway. 
(That was Joe C. Lawrence's idea.)
     I think it's important that all pets still respect curses, and for
that matter, I see no reason why they shouldn't do it all the time (except
for underlying food, of course); it seems like a pretty dumb way to lose
a character that your pet, normally a reliable check, suddenly decides
it's going to ignore its instincts.
     And, of course, bigger pets would cost more money.  Make sure all
player classes start with some 100gp more than they do previously (enough
for a median-sized pet, most likely) and I believe someone's mentioned
pet shops.  There, for instance, for a goodly 5000gp, you might be able to
get ahold of a jabberwocky-sized dog (you can tame a jabberwocky, I believe,
so this wouldn't overbalance the game too much).  Naturally, move_pet
would *not* work on this beast.
     A sample list:
Dogs                                  Cats
Toy dog 10gp  1HD (3 max), 4hp (20)   Small cat-- hp 1(3), hp 4(20)
Poodle  25gp  2HD (4 max), 6hp (30)   Slightly less small cat (etc.)


